The code does not compile because `std::ostream` (the type of `std::cout`) does not have an overloaded `operator<<` that knows how to output a custom struct like `Point`. The standard library only provides overloads for built-in types (like `int`, `float`, `char*`, etc.) and some standard library types (like `std::string`).

To fix this, you need to define a custom `operator<<` for your `Point` struct. The most common and idiomatic way to do this is to define a free (non-member) function that takes an `std::ostream` reference and a `const Point&`, and returns the `std::ostream` reference so that multiple insertions can be chained.

Here is the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    int x, y;
};

// Overload operator<< for Point
std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Point& p) {
    os << "Point(" << p.x << ", " << p.y << ")";
    return os;
}

int main() {
    Point p{1, 2};
    std::cout << p << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

